  requirements you don't have — no second storage backend exists, and no component currently needs to react to consent
  changing mid-session.
  [3mWins when:[0m server identity is already scheduled, or consent can be revoked from several places at once.
  [3mComplexity:[0m high, and mostly speculative.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It fits the codebase as it actually is, and the interface — five functions — is identical in all
  three. That's the part other forms depend on, so choosing A now doesn't make B or C harder later; you'd be changing
  how the module is loaded, not what it does.

  [1mOne data-model choice inside whichever you pick:[0m store consent and id as a [3msingle[0m versioned JSON blob under one key
  ([38;5;153m{v: 1, consent: true, userId: "...", createdAt: "..."}[39m) rather than two separate keys. Two keys can desync — consent
  revoked while an id lingers, which is the exact failure a consent gate exists to prevent — and one key gives a single
  place to handle format changes. I'd fold this into the design unless you object.

  [1mTwo consequences worth naming now:[0m [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m when consent hasn't been granted, so [38;5;153mlogin[39m must treat a
  missing id as ordinary, not an error — it logs without one. And id generation uses [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m, which needs a
  secure context; I'll spec a fallback so opening the page from disk doesn't break.

  [1mTooling:[0m this repo has no linter, formatter, or test framework, and nothing in it is currently tested. Consent logic
  that's wrong fails silently and in the user's favor least — it's worth a test. Cheapest to set up now, before the code
  exists.
